{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791568418,"nonce":"2yxwtwNdlHEWSUuIn1+hQd2xZFw=","message":"Shared root cause, second finding: a value whose type is only known through a guard stayed a boxed `val` in the compile clone, so loops seeded from list elements or arguments ran generic compares and arithmetic.\n\nLeaks found and closed (uncommitted at the time of writing, NUC x86-64, taskset 4-7, medians):\n- Num `==`/`!=` had no f64 form: added CmpEqF64/CmpNeF64 (IEEE: -0 == 0, NaN != NaN), specialised by TypeSpecialize.\n- Arguments of a function reached through OSR were never profiled (only compiled entries profile args): the interpreter now ors argument kinds into result_kinds when it reaches an OSR point.\n- UnboxParams ignored guarded values as f64 sources; now a guarded value seeds a raw loop variable, while a parameter that only passes a guarded value along stays boxed (as f64 it held a float register across nbody's loops: +15% steady).\n- OSR layouts rejected any f64 live-in that was not a block param (an unbox LICM hoisted, or the f64 behind a box), so guarding `n` cost fasta/k_nucleotide their loop entries: entries now rebuild such live-ins from the interpreter register of the boxed value (bounded by `interp_registers`, the original function's value count).\n- Cranelift OSR entries jumped straight to the loop header, so every entry-defined value became a header param, loop-variant to Cranelift's LICM. Entries now go through a landing block before the header that the body's own outside edges also take.\n- LLVM entries with unboxed live-ins cost nbody's advance (short calls) 11-15% steady. TierCell now counts compiled entries; the LLVM tier keeps unboxed live-ins only when counted iterations per call \u003e= 32.\n\nList stores: x86-64 LLVM write was 3.7 ns/elem (LuaJIT 0.43) because each store folded elem_class through a loop-carried load/cmov/store. A known Num stores the MIXED constant, and a MIXED word skips the fold (MIXED absorbs). LLVM write 0.66-0.79 ns on x86-64, 0.61 on arm64 (LuaJIT 0.61); swap 5.0 -\u003e 3.0 ns x86, 3.4 -\u003e 2.3 arm64.\n\nHead vs all changes (x86-64, default tiers, pass1 / steady seconds):\nfannkuch_redux 0.968/0.861 -\u003e 0.715/0.625\nspectral_norm 0.049/0.0153 -\u003e 0.039/0.0133\nnbody, fasta, k_nucleotide, reverse_complement, mandelbrot, pidigits, regex_redux: level within noise (NUC load 4-7 from other sessions).\n\nStill open from this root cause:\n- frequencies() in k_nucleotide: `key` stays generic because the k-iteration seed loop's dna[j] read gets no speculation guard (no profile at that site).\n- Interpreter OSR into frequencies declines thousands of times on \"live-in v53 undefined\": RangeLoop reuses a value id with a new meaning, so the register the entry asks for was never written. Predates this work.\n- Cranelift x86-64 list read/write still 2.1-2.2 ns vs LuaJIT 0.65/0.43.","files":null}]}